  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nSpotty light showers Sunday\n\nUpper wave currently over Saskatchewan looks to dive southeast over \nLake Superior Sunday. In addition to helping advance the cold front \nmoving through the forecast area today this may also kick off a few \nvery light showers as the mid-levels will still have a decent amount \nof moisture remaining in them as the base of the upper wave moves \nthrough the forecast area. Have therefore added 15-25% mentions of \nlight showers with very low QPF. Those outdoors shouldn't be \nsurprised if rain occurs briefly but impacts are not expected.\n\nRain with localized flooding risk Monday into Tuesday\n\nIn the wake of Sunday's upper wave, surface high builds in the Great \nLakes. First bona fide Fall-like temperatures look to occur Monday \nmorning when some areas in our northeast could (20-50%, highest in \nTaylor County) see temperatures in the upper 30s.\n\nAs this upper wave continues to head east over the Atlantic Ocean, \nsurface high shifts eastward accordingly. As this occurs, upper low \nchurns eastward over MT toward southern SK/MB. Strong southwesterly \njet aloft on the equatar-ward side of this feature will both lead to \nthe low deepening and broad lee surface pressure falls across the \ncentral and northern High Plains. Consequently, robust southerly \nmoist advection will occur between the eastern surface high and the \nlee trough across the Plains with 700/850mb moisture transport \nramping up over our CWA Monday morning. Expect showers and perhaps a \nfew thunderstorms to develop as a pair of disturbances eject \ndownstream during the afternoon and evening hours and then again \nwhen the surface front arrives from the northwest early Tuesday. \nWith high PWAT values expected (12.13z NBM values approach 2\") and \ndeep warm cloud layers, showers will produce rain efficiently with a \ngood chance (50-70%) for rain totals to exceed 2\". Given the setup \nwith 1-2 round of moderate to heavy rain developing in the vicinity \nof a warm front before additional rain occurs with the crashing cold \nfront, there exists potential for localized high end amounts with \nthe small (5-15%) chance for 4\"+ to occur focusing on portions of \nnortheast Iowa. Thus, while the progressive nature of the overall \nsystem and probabilistic output suggest little risk for widespread \nflooding issues, the potential for localized problems looks to be \npresent.\n\nVery low severe storm potential Monday\n\nWith the aforementioned southwesterly jet sliding closer to the CWA \nby Monday afternoon/evening and southerly winds ramping up at the \nsurface, oodles of wind shear look to be present, including at the \nnear surface, where sfc-500m SRH could top 200 m2/s2 per 12.12z GFS. \nHowever, given the strong moist advection, destabilization will be \nan issue as increasing clouds limit insolation and profiles begin to \ntrend moist adiabatic. Therefore, while we cannot let our guard down \nas even modest amounts of surface-based instability would be enough \nfor a tornado risk, at this time it appears highly unlikely (95%) \nsaid instability will in fact develop.\n\nPeriodic rain Wednesday through Friday\n\nWith moist of the upper support for they Monday/Tuesday system \nremaining to our north, cold front looks to stall across NE/IA/IL \nwith quasi-zonal flow aloft persisting over the northern CONUS. \nThus, as shortwaves move across the Midwest, additional rounds of \nrain look to develop in the vicinity of the front. Some of this \nactivity may (20-60%) sneak into our CWA, particularly in northeast \nIowa and southwest Wisconsin. While no additional hazardous periods \nstick out at this time, would need to monitor any areas that receive \nheavy rain Monday/Tuesday for additional flooding concerns.",
